window.docContents[156].resolve({"/ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics/The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-linear-acoustics-built-by-the-kind-algebra/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics--The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-linear-acoustics-built-by-the-kind-algebra":{"contents":"Several Part-8 definitions state a quantity's defining relation algebraically — the\nconstitutive laws of linear acoustics. Sound particle velocity is the time-derivative of\ndisplacement (8-4), acceleration of velocity (8-5); sound intensity is the product of\nsound pressure and particle velocity (8-10, I = p·u); the two impedances are quotients\nof sound pressure (8-12, 8-13). Each is specified as an R12 kind-law, the kinematics\ncrossing to ISO 80000-3 for time.\n\n\n\nSound intensity is sound pressure × particle velocity — a verified construction (item\n8-10). A sound intensity built as the product of a sound pressure and a particle velocity\ncarries its product certificate by construction, over the ℝ carrier. The companion\nsoundIntensity_dim_from_pressure_velocity shows the intensity is M·T⁻³ because the\nlength in pressure and velocity cancels — the acoustic intensity law as a checked\ncomputation. Uses the quotient kind-law.\n\nsoundIntensityOf p u := Quantity.mul soundIntensity_prod_pressure_velocity p u, and\nsoundIntensityOf_isProduct is rfl. The same family gives the quotient constructors\ncharacteristicImpedanceOf (= p/u) and the cross-part kinematic laws\nparticleVelocity_quot_displacement_duration (= displacement / time), the time crossing\nto ISO 80000-3.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-8 — Acoustics","header":"27.4. The algebraic Remarks as kind-laws: linear acoustics built by the kind algebra","id":"/ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics/The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-linear-acoustics-built-by-the-kind-algebra/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-8-___-Acoustics--The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-linear-acoustics-built-by-the-kind-algebra"},"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/Six-demands-on-the-framework/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--Six-demands-on-the-framework":{"contents":"Each demand follows from the write-once discipline. None of them is particular to one model.\n\n* Demand* Why the calculus needs it* What carries it\n* One source, many carriers* A model is authored once over [NumCarrier α] and instantiated along the carrier ladder: ℝ for proof, binary32 as the rounding specification, the executable binary32, Float, an eager device carrier, and the recording carrier. Each rung asks the framework for a different thing: proved derivatives at ℝ, executable floating-point facts at binary32, the whole operation set on device buffers, and a recorder.* TorchLean's scalar-generic Context and MathFunctions, which PropertyKindCalculus's MathCarrier mirrors field for field; the forwarding instances in PropertyKindCalculus.Torch.Paradigm.NumCarrierContext\n* A bit-exact oracle on every machine* The single-threaded Float run is the reference every other carrier is checked against. The acceptance rule is stated per check: bit for bit where both sides are IEEE-exact at one precision (the table fetch, where a NaN matches any NaN; an emitted kernel's outputs whose cone reaches no exp or log); within an ulp bound where a library exp or log is reached (four ulps by default in julia_parity); within a relative tolerance on every output for the KernelAbstractions twin of an emitted kernel (1e-9 by default); and within a stated tolerance (1e-3) where a binary32 batched run is compared with the binary64 reference. That check has to run where there is no GPU (continuous integration, a CPU-only deployment), and a disagreement between devices is a defect to find, not noise to tolerate.* The LibTorch bridge running on the host through ATen's CPU kernels; the parity executables ssprc_batched_parity, mcm_batched_parity and lut_cudat_parity\n* Proofs whose cost does not grow with the model* A science model has hundreds of operations. A parity proof written per operation per model would not be written at all.* The denotation bridge, preserved by each carrier operation once, bundled as a homomorphism and closed per model by the tape_hom tactic, whose residual is the list of the model's own definitions to unfold; the kind-transporting weak bisimulation, whose acceptance is decided, not proved, per model\n* A recording that costs what the model costs* The megakernel path records the whole model, so a recorder whose cost grows faster than the number of operations makes the path unusable on exactly the models that need it.* TorchLean's Tape.record, which pushes each node in place; the compacted tape, which merges identical sub-expressions without moving a value; the sharing recorder SharedTapeBuilder, which records the compacted tape directly and never holds the expression tree, with shares_record proving it is the compaction of the unshared recording\n* Arithmetic intensity* Eager device execution round-trips every intermediate through device memory, so it is memory-bound. Fusion into one kernel keeps intermediates in registers. Tabulated data enters as a declared vocabulary extension whose device form is one kernel too.* The straight-line program read off a tape and printed as CUDA, C or Julia; TorchLean's certified kernel compiler for the table fetch\n* Metrological meaning survives the acceleration* A fused kernel carries no kinds. If its inputs, constants and kind changes cannot be tied back to the source's, the speed is bought with the provenance.* The kind-transporting weak bisimulation between the metrological provenance hypergraph and the computational tape graph, and the seal of the computation over it\n\nThe last demand is the one a metrologist asks about first, and the one no other demand settles.\nThe relation it rests on is a weak bisimulation, which is stronger than a refinement: a\nrefinement would say that every computation step corresponds to a step the source allows, and\nthe bisimulation says that, in both directions. Every occurrence the hypergraph states is\nrealized on the tape, and every tape path projects to a hypergraph path. It is weak because\nit says nothing about the inside of one operation's expansion on the carrier, which is where\nthe kinds are erased anyway.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.1. Six demands on the framework","id":"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/Six-demands-on-the-framework/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--Six-demands-on-the-framework"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--System-context-card":{"contents":"* Scope* System or component* Sort / status* L2 assumptions and attributes\n* System* Coupled pair* pairS; composite whole* Bodies A and B interact through kC. The normal-mode angular frequency belongs to the pair.\n* Part* Body A* Body.A, sort bodyS* Homogeneous, rigid, point-like; mass mA lies on the spring axis.\n* Part* Body B* Body.B, sort bodyS* Homogeneous, rigid, point-like; mass mB lies on the spring axis.\n* Part* Wall spring kA* Spring.kA, sort springS* Linear spring from body A to the fixed wall; stiffness kA and signed extension (negative compressed, positive stretched).\n* Part* Coupling spring kC* Spring.kC, sort springS* Linear spring joining bodies A and B; stiffness kC and signed extension (negative compressed, positive stretched).\n* Part* Wall spring kB* Spring.kB, sort springS* Linear spring from body B to the fixed wall; stiffness kB and signed extension (negative compressed, positive stretched).\n* Outside* Walls and line* Fixed, infinitely massive walls; one-dimensional motion* The wall springs remain attached to fixed boundaries and both bodies move along the line.\n\nThe Lean model keeps each body's oscillator as the source of its mass and wall-spring\nstiffness; kC is the pair's new coupling field, and extensionOf indexes each signed\ndisplacement by its spring part. One PairId name is one system: records under it are states\nat different times, configurations, or rival estimates to compare; distinct names are\ndistinct systems.\nPartOf π uses nominal identity, so identical parameter records under P and Q still yield\ndifferent pair part types. The Measurement\nnumeral is an Int: the series example is exact for kA–kB (6 ∥ 3 = 2), but these integer\nnumerals do not model longer chains. L3 revisits the assumption that each body is rigid,\nhomogeneous, and point-like with its mass on the spring axis: if the mass is off-axis, the\nbody wobbles.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.10. System-context card","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--System-context-card"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"Optional. Add a viscous damper to oscillator A, so that its energy is no longer conserved. Three quantities are easy to confuse here: PhysLib's damping constant γ (kg/s); ISO 80000-3's damping coefficient δ = γ/(2m) (s⁻¹), which PhysLib calls the decay rate; and the angular frequency ω₀ (rad/s), which has the same dimension as δ. The lesson gives each its own kind, so adding δ to ω₀ fails to type-check. decayRateOf computes δ from one oscillator's γ and mass with IndividualQuantity.div, so oscillator B's mass cannot be used for oscillator A's δ.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L5 — The oscillation dies out\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.6.1.1. Purpose","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--Synopsis--Purpose"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--System-context-card":{"contents":"* Scope* System or component* Sort / status* L6 requirements\n* System* Coupled pair P or Q* Nominal PairId, sorted into pairS* One name denotes one pair; same-name records are states or configurations, and Q differs from P.\n* Part* Body A or B* Designated body* Each body has its own mass bearer within the named pair.\n* Part* Coupling spring kC* Designated spring* Its extension is state-indexed and checked against an external rated limit.\n* Requirement* Pair mass R1* pairS sort* Provable from the pair's two body masses; spot check gives 8 from 3 and 5.\n* Requirement* Normal-mode frequency R2* pairS sort* Provable negative: no aggregation license for normalModeK.\n* Requirement* Spring range R3* Singleton object scope containing P* Empirical, gated by the datasheet referent; applies to every P state or record.\n* Audit* Requirement census* Stage 4* The valid requirements govern every produced model port; invalid-scope twins are counterexamples.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.10. System-context card","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--System-context-card"},"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/What-a-vocabulary-answers-for-___-the-coverage-censuses/#annotation-kindDiagnostic":{"contents":"Not an exception but an enrollment, on a KindOfProperty: the kind is a quality or\nconditioning output a consumer must read, and the string says what it diagnoses.\n\n/-- A quality kind the retrieval exports: marked diagnostic, and carried by a produced\nport of `Good.invBoundary` below. -/\n@[kindDiagnostic \"whether the retrieval's answer was decided inside its domain\"]\ndef qcK : KindOfProperty := { id := \"contract coverage probe quality\", scale := .ordinal }\n\n\n#kind_diagnostic_coverage then asks the declared boundaries in scope for a produced port\ncarrying it; a marked kind no boundary exports is a side channel. An unmarked kind is not\nwalked, so removing the mark is the exemption, and a scope with no marks records that\nvisibly. Rejected at elaboration unless the declaration is a KindOfProperty and the\ndescription is non-empty.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009What a vocabulary answers for — the coverage censuses","header":"35.7.4. @[kindDiagnostic \"…\"]  — a conditioning output, enrolled","id":"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/What-a-vocabulary-answers-for-___-the-coverage-censuses/#annotation-kindDiagnostic"}});